

===== STATUS: success =====
Question: What retrieval-augmented methods for language models were proposed in 2020 or 2021?

RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) is a retrieval-augmented method for language models proposed in 2020-2021 (Lewis2005 pages 7-9). The architecture combines a dense neural retriever with a pre-trained encoder-decoder generative language model, specifically BART or T5 (Lewis2005 pages 7-9).

RAG incorporates several key innovations. First, it employs learned retrieval that outperforms fixed BM25 retrieval across multiple NLP tasks (Lewis2005 pages 7-9). Second, the method features non-parametric memory, enabling straightforward knowledge updates at test time through document index swapping without retraining (Lewis2005 pages 7-9). Third, it provides flexibility to adjust retrieved documents during inference (Lewis2005 pages 7-9).

RAG demonstrates strong performance across diverse knowledge-intensive NLP tasks, including open-domain question answering, fact-checking (FEVER), question generation, and dialogue systems (Lewis2005 pages 7-9). The unified retrieval-based architecture achieves competitive results across multiple tasks, establishing RAG as an effective approach for knowledge-intensive NLP applications (Lewis2005 pages 7-9).

References

1. (Lewis2005 pages 7-9): Lewis, Patrick, et al. "Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks." arXiv preprint arXiv:2005.11401v4, 12 Apr. 2021, arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401.
